.SH DESCRIPTION
.B openre
is one static Rust binary that does two jobs security teams usually buy
separate tools for: it scans websites and APIs for vulnerabilities, and it
analyzes compiled binaries (ELF, PE, Mach-O, WASM) down to symbols,
imports, sections, disassembly, control-flow graphs, and dataflow. The
container format is detected from the file's bytes; no database, server,
or Docker is involved, and scan history lives in a SQLite that is compiled
into the binary, so there is no database to install. TLS goes through the
system OpenSSL on the glibc build (reqwest's default TLS), so an install
is the one file plus a libc.
.PP
The scan side ships 18 built-in web checks (see docs/checks.md) grouped
into three profiles: quick (6 checks, a CI-gate-sized pass), standard
(16), and full (18). Every scan is recorded to local SQLite history,
findings print as they are discovered, and output is machine-readable on
request: JSON, YAML, or SARIF for GitHub code scanning. Scans can be
paused and resumed by ID, exported and imported (findings, scan
summaries, projects), and scheduled for continuous monitoring.
.PP
The analysis side is a capstone-backed disassembler with a multi-stage
pipeline: identify the format, load, disassemble, recover a CFG, run
dataflow, recover types, decompile, and enrich. Stages are selectable
with
.B \-\-stages
as a comma-separated list.
.PP
An optional AI research layer correlates findings, explains them, drafts
remediation plans, and generates proof-of-concept exploits. It is
local-first: no provider is configured by default, and with none set the
.B ai
commands say so and everything else stays fully functional. Remote
providers (OpenAI, Anthropic, vLLM) are opt-in through configuration.
Exploit generation and remediation guidance work from the built-in
template and knowledge bases with no provider at all.
.PP
.B hunt
chains the pieces: scan a target, analyze what came back, generate PoCs
for the critical findings, and write a shareable HTML report
(openre-hunt-report.html in the working directory).
.SH COMMANDS
.SS openre hunt
.B openre hunt
.B \-t
.IR TARGET ...
.PP
One-shot assessment of a URL, IP, hostname, or file path: scan, analyze,
generate PoCs, write the report. Depth is
.B quick
or
.B standard
(default) or
.BR deep .
Format is
.B terminal
(default) or
.B json
or
.B yaml
or
.BR html .
.B \-o
.RB ( \-\-open )
tries to open the report in a browser;
.B \-T
.RB ( \-\-technical )
adds technical detail to the output;
.B \-v
.RB ( \-\-viral )
and
.B \-a
.RB ( \-\-achievements )
add share badges and achievement tracking;
.B \-O
.I DIR
redirects the report to a directory;
.B \-y
skips confirmation prompts.
.SS openre scan quick|standard|full
.B openre scan
.I PROFILE
.I TARGET
.RI [ options ]
.PP
Run the named profile against one target. Profiles and their timing
(quick about 2-3s, standard 10-15s, full 30-60s on the fixture server)
come from the 18 checks in docs/checks.md. Findings print as they land;
the summary block tallies them by severity.
.B \-\-header "KEY: VALUE"
is repeatable and adds request headers;
.B \-\-user\-agent
overrides the UA string;
.B \-\-proxy
routes requests through a proxy;
.B \-\-rate\-limit
caps requests per second;
.B \-\-timeout
(default 30) and
.B \-\-max\-redirects
(default 10) bound each request;
.B \-\-follow\-redirects
(default true) toggles redirect following.
.B \-\-checks
and
.B \-\-exclude
take comma-separated check names from docs/checks.md and filter the
profile's list. When neither is passed, the built-in
.B sensitive-files
check is left out to keep default scans quiet.
.B \-o
writes the result to a file; a
.I .sarif
extension selects SARIF, a
.I .json
extension selects JSON, otherwise the configured default format is used.
.PP
Every scan is saved to SQLite history before, during, and after, so
another process (or another terminal) can drive it:
.SS openre scan pause / resume / custom
.B openre scan pause
.I SCAN_ID
.br
.B openre scan resume
.I SCAN_ID
.PP
Flip a running scan's status to paused (the scan loop polls between
checks and sleeps while paused) and back. Cancelling or failing is
visible in
.B openre queue
history but has no dedicated subcommand.
.B openre scan custom
is the explicit form: pick
.B \-\-profile
(default standard) plus
.B \-\-checks
/
.B \-\-exclude
lists yourself.
.SS openre scan export / import
.B openre scan export findings
.I SCAN_ID
.RB [ \-\-format
.RI { json | csv }]
.RB [ \-o
.IR FILE ]
.br
.B openre scan export scans
.RB [ \-\-project\-id
.IR ID ]
.RB [ \-\-status
.IR STATUS ]
.RB [ \-\-date\-from
.IR ISO8601 ]
.RB [ \-\-date\-to
.IR ISO8601 ]
.RB [ \-\-tags
.IR LIST ]
.RB [ \-\-min\-severity
.IR SEV ]
.RB [ \-\-format
.RI { json | csv }]
.RB [ \-o
.IR FILE ]
.br
.B openre scan export projects
.RB [ \-\-format
.RI { json | csv }]
.RB [ \-o
.IR FILE ]
.br
.B openre scan import findings
.I INPUT
.B \-\-scan\-id
.IR SCAN_ID
.br
.B openre scan import scans
.I INPUT
.br
.B openre scan import projects
.I INPUT
.PP
Exports read from history; without
.BR \-o ,
they print to stdout. Imports read JSON (or the CSV shape the exports
write) and file the rows back into history; importing findings requires
the target scan to exist.
.SS openre analyze
.B openre analyze
.I SUBCOMMAND
.I FILE
.RI [ options ]
.PP
The binary-analysis half. The target is a file on disk; the format is
auto-detected and
.B \-\-binary\-format
(elf, pe, macho, wasm, auto) overrides detection when needed. Every
subcommand accepts
.B \-\-limit
(0 = unlimited; default 100 rows) and
.BR \-o .
.TP
.B info
Identify the container and print its metadata.
.TP
.B symbols\ imports\ exports
List the symbol table, dynamic imports, and exports.
.TP
.B strings
Extract printable ASCII runs; \-l sets the minimum length (default 4).
.TP
.B sections\ segments
List the section table and program headers.
.TP
.B functions
List functions from the symbol table;
.B \-f
filters by name pattern,
.B \-\-details
adds type, binding, and visibility.
.TP
.B disasm
Disassemble a function (\-f name or address) or an address range
.RB ( \-\-start / \-\-end ,
hex);
.B \-c
caps the instruction count (default 50),
.B \-\-bytes
adds the raw bytes column.
.TP
.B decompile
Decompile one function (\-f, required).
.TP
.B pipeline
Run the analysis pipeline;
.B \-\-stages
is a comma-separated list of identify, load, disassemble, cfg, dataflow,
types, decompile, ai-enrich, or all (the default).
.SS openre ai
.B openre ai
.I SUBCOMMAND
.RI [ args ]
.PP
The AI research layer over findings and binaries. With no provider
configured in the config file the chat command says so and exits;
.B ai providers
lists what is available and
.B ai test
checks one. Findings are addressed by ID (from history) or by a JSON
file path.
.B chat
sends one message or starts an interactive session;
.B analyze
and
.B explain
break a finding down (explain's detail/audience flags tune the answer
for the reader);
.B remediate
drafts a fix;
.B correlate
links findings across a project or results directory
.RB ( \-\-min\-confidence ,
default 0.5);
.B \-\-ultrathink
raises the thinking budget on
.BR analyze .
.SS openre exploit
.B openre exploit generate
.I FINDING
.RI [ options ]
.PP
Generate a proof-of-concept exploit for a finding from the built-in
template set. Languages: python (default), bash, javascript, go, rust,
ruby, php, java, csharp, powershell.
.B \-\-template
picks a specific template,
.B \-\-target
overrides the URL the PoC points at,
.B \-\-safe
(default true) keeps destructive payloads out.
.B exploit templates
lists what ships (filter by
.B \-\-category
\-\- sqli, xss, ssrf, xxe, command-injection, deserialization,
auth-bypass, idor, csrf, open-redirect \-\- or by language),
.B exploit show
prints one template,
.B exploit validate
checks a generated exploit file against a target without firing it
unless it is not
.BR \-\-dry\-run .
.SS openre remediate
.B openre remediate
.I SUBCOMMAND
.I FINDING
.RI [ options ]
.PP
Fix guidance, verified against the knowledge base.
.B plan
produces a full remediation plan for an environment (development,
staging, production \-\- the default \-\- or ci-cd), optionally mapped to
a compliance framework (owasp, pci-dss, hipaa, gdpr, soc2, iso27001,
nist).
.B quick\-fix
is the one-patch version for a language (python, javascript, typescript,
go, java, csharp, php, ruby, rust).
.B report
aggregates every finding JSON in a directory into a remediation report,
grouped by severity (default), category, component, or compliance.
.B verify
checks whether the finding still reproduces against an optional target.
.SS openre report
.B openre report
.I SUBCOMMAND
.RI [ args ]
.PP
Report template management.
.B list
paginates stored templates,
.B create
stores one (\-n name, \-f format among markdown, html, json, sarif;
\-c content, or a path with \-\-from\-file),
.B delete
removes it by name, and
.B use
renders a stored template for one scan (\-s scan ID), writing to
.B \-o
when given. Templates live in the SQLite history store.
.SS openre config
.B openre config
.I SUBCOMMAND
.RI [ args ]
.PP
Inspect and manage configuration.
.B show
prints the effective config (a
.B \-\-section
narrows it,
.B \-\-json
makes it machine-readable),
.B set/get
read and write one dotted key (ai.provider, scan.timeout, ...),
.B reset
restores defaults for the file or one section
.RB ( \-\-yes
skips the prompt),
.B path
prints the config file location,
.B edit
opens it in $EDITOR (default vi), and
.B init
writes the default file
.RB ( \-\-force
overwrites).
.SS openre queue
.B openre queue
.I SUBCOMMAND
.RI [ args ]
.PP
A local job queue over the same SQLite store; the analysis pipeline is
what executes jobs, this tracks submissions, state, and logs.
.B submit
takes a payload (inline JSON, or @file), a job type (analysis by
default; identification, disassembly, control-flow, data-flow,
type-recovery, decompilation, ai-enrichment, export, import,
plugin-execution), a priority (low, default, high; \-P), an optional
RFC3339 \-\-schedule\-at, retry bounds (\-\-max\-attempts, default 3), and
optional project/file/user IDs, timeout, and repeated key=value
\-\-tag rows.
.B status
shows one job,
.B cancel
stops a pending or running one (\-\-reason is recorded),
.B retry
requeues a failed one,
.B logs
prints a job's log entries (\-F follows),
.B stats
summarizes the queue, and
.B list
filters by status, type, priority, or project. All of these accept
.B \-f
for table (default), json, or yaml.
.SS openre schedule
.B openre schedule
.I SUBCOMMAND
.RI [ args ]
.PP
Scheduled scans for continuous monitoring, stored in their own SQLite
file.
.B add
takes a target, \-n name, \-\-profile (default standard), and \-i
interval in minutes (default 60), plus the scan knobs (\-\-checks,
\-\-exclude, repeatable \-\-header, \-\-timeout, \-\-follow\-redirects,
\-\-max\-redirects, \-\-user\-agent, \-\-proxy, \-\-rate\-limit,
\-\-no\-tls\-verify).
.B list
shows schedules (\-\-enabled\-only / \-\-disabled\-only filter),
.B remove/pause/resume/show
act on a schedule ID, and
.B daemon
runs the scheduler loop that fires schedules when due (\-\-once for a
single pass, the way a service manager would call it).
.SS openre completions
.B openre completions
.RB { bash | zsh | fish | powershell }
.PP
Print a shell completion script to stdout. It covers the subcommands and
flags of the build that produced it, so regenerate it after upgrading:
.TP
bash
.B openre completions bash
> ~/.local/share/bash-completion/completions/openre
.TP
zsh
.B openre completions zsh
> ~/.zfunc/_openre (put ~/.zfunc on your fpath before compinit)
.TP
fish
.B openre completions fish
> ~/.config/fish/completions/openre.fish
.TP
powershell
.B openre completions powershell
>> $PROFILE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/openre/config.toml
The user configuration. Resolution order (later sources override
earlier ones): compiled defaults, this file,
.I config.local.toml
(gitignored overrides),
.I OPENRE_*
environment variables
.RB ( "__"
separates sections, e.g. OPENRE_AI__PROVIDER),
and
.I config.local.json
last. On macOS the directory is ~/Library/Application Support/openre, on
Windows %APPDATA%\\openre. No config file is required: defaults are
compiled in.
.TP
.I ./data/storage/history.db
SQLite scan history: scan summaries, findings, deduplicated findings,
and report templates. The path comes from the storage.local_path
configuration key, default ./data/storage relative to the directory
openre runs in.
.TP
.I ./data/storage/schedules.db
The schedule store used by
.B openre schedule.
.TP
.I ./openre-hunt-report.html
The report
.B openre hunt
writes into the working directory.
.TP
.I config.toml
A sample configuration ships in the repository root for development.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
success
.TP
.B 1
runtime error (config, scan, storage, or IO failure, scan cancelled or
failed, target not found)
.TP
.B 2
usage error (unknown flag, missing argument, rejected shell name)
.SH SEE ALSO
Project documentation ships in the repository under docs/: cli.md (the
full CLI reference this page summarizes), checks.md (the 18 checks and
their profiles), architecture.md, sarif.md, ai-providers.md, and
PERFORMANCE.md.
.PP
